           <div>
               <head xml:id="intro">Introduction</head>
               <p>This Guide provides a brief summary of how to create letter transcriptions that
                    will work in <ref target="https://epsilon.ac.uk">Epsilon</ref>. It is not
                    intended to provide a comprehensive introduction to XML or the Text Encoding
                    Initiative Guidelines. There are <ref>other, better, sources of information on
                        this</ref>. To make sense of this Guide, you will need to become familiar
                    with certain key XML concepts, like <hi rend="italic">element</hi>, the
                    difference between <hi rend="italic">empty</hi> and <hi rend="italic"
                        >non-empty</hi> elements, <hi rend="italic">attributes</hi>, <hi
                        rend="italic">attribute values</hi>, <hi rend="italic">entities</hi>, and
                        <hi rend="italic">validation</hi>.</p>
                   <p><hi rend="bold">Elements</hi> are what most people would likely call tags,
                    such as <gi>p</gi>, the tag that is used to denote paragraphs.</p>
               <p><hi rend="bold">Attributes</hi> are used to define the characteristics of the
                    element to which they are attached. In the following example, <tag>p
                        rend="indent0"</tag>, ‘p’ (the element) has a ‘rend’ attribute (usually
                    depicted as <att>rend</att> within documentation) with a value of
                        ‘<val>indent0</val>’. In Epsilon, that means that the paragraph will not
                    have the first line indented.</p>
                <p><hi rend="bold">Entities</hi> are the chief method used to encode certain special
                    characters within a document. A list of these characters is included in Appendix
                    XXXX.</p>
                <p><hi rend="bold">XML Comments</hi> are used to provide comments on the
                    transcription code. They look like <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xmlns:tei="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:teix="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xml:space="preserve"><![CDATA[<!-- I am unable to read this word. Jane, can you make it out? -->]]></egXML> They are not visible on the public site but are useful for give contextual information to the editor. You will most likely encounter them in the Epsilon letter template file, where they provide an explanation of the information needed for certain mandatory fields.</p>
                <p>It is also important that you understand the principle of <hi rend="italic"
                        >nesting</hi> elements. These concepts are not difficult to grasp and are
                    explained in any guide to XML. Newcomers to the language may find the Text
                    Encoding Initiative’s ‘<ref
                        target="http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SG.html">Gentle
                        Introduction to XML</ref>’ useful.</p>
               <p>We recommend that anyone wishing to transcribe a letter creates it using a
                    duplicate of our <val>letter_template.xml</val> file. This document includes all
                    the basic structure that's required for your transcription along with XML
                    comments highlighting where you need to update content.</p>
           </div>
           <div>
               <head xml:id="documentID">Filenames and Document IDs</head> 
               <p>Every Epsilon letter file requires a unique document ID to distinguish it from
                   every other letter in the collection. We recommend including a shortened form of
                   the collection's name in the ID. For instance, if transcribing letters of Mary
                   Anning, suitable IDs might be: ‘<val>ANNING-LETTER-1</val>’,
                   ‘<val>ANNING-LETTER-2</val>’, etc. The files should have the same base name
                   as their ID value, <hi rend="italic">e.g.</hi> ‘<val>ANNING-LETTER-1.xml</val>’,
                   ‘<val>ANNING-LETTER-2.xml</val>’, and so forth. </p>
               <p>The document's ID is recorded on the <gi>TEI</gi> element’s <att>xml:id</att>
                   attribute, like this: <tag>TEI xml:id="ANNING-LETTER-1"</tag>.</p> 
           </div>
           <div>
               <head xml:id="schema">The Epsilon Schema</head>
               <p>The Text Encoding Initiative Guidelines are extensive and contain many elements
                    that are unnecessary or irrelevant for letter transcriptions used in Epsilon.
                    For this reason, we have created a customized schema specifically tailored to
                    Epsilon that only includes the elements and attributes directly relevant to
                    Epsilon. Our schema also contains extra validation rules to check that all
                    essential data is included in your transcription.</p>
               <p>If you create your document from a copy of the <val>letter_template.xml</val> file
                    in this repository, it will automatically use the Epsilon schema. </p>
                <p>If you should happen to create a TEI file on your own, you can add the following
                    two lines immediately before the <gi>TEI</gi> element to tell it to
                    automatically use the Epsilon schema.</p>
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xmlns:tei="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:teix="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" xml:space="preserve">
<?xml-model href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cambridge-collection/epsilon-tei-odd/refs/heads/main/rng/letters.rng" type="application/xml" schematypens="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0"?>
<?xml-model href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cambridge-collection/epsilon-tei-odd/refs/heads/main/rng/letters.rng" type="application/xml" schematypens="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron"?>
</egXML>
           </div>
           <div> 
               <head xml:id="fileParts">The Main Parts of the Letter File</head>
               <p>Every Epsilon letter file consists of two sections:</p>
               <list rend="ordered">
                   <item><gi>teiHeader</gi> contains all the metadata about the source document and its electronic version.</item>
                   <item><gi>text</gi> contains the textual content of the letter and any editorial footnotes.</item>
               </list>
           </div>
           
           

           <div>
               <head xml:id="teiHeader">teiHeader</head>
               <p><gi>teiHeader</gi> contains three elements that must appear in the following order:</p>
               <list>
                   <item><gi>fileDesc</gi></item>
                   <item><gi>profileDesc</gi></item>
                   <item><gi>revisionDesc</gi></item>
               </list>
               <div>
                   <head xml:id="fileDesc">fileDesc</head>
                   <p><gi>fileDesc</gi> contains information about the electronic file and the
                        physical letter’s source(s). This includes:</p>
                   
                   <div>
                        <head xml:id="titleStmt">The Title [REQ]</head>
                        <p>Every TEI document must have a <gi>title</gi> element. However, since
                            Epsilon does not use this element directly, you have flexibility in
                            choosing the title format. You can provide either a descriptive,
                            older-style title, such as:</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><titleStmt><title>Letter to Charlotte Murchison, 25 Feb [1829]</title></titleStmt></egXML>
                        <p>or a shorter style version that reflects how the letter actually appears
                            in Epsilon:</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><titleStmt><title xml:id="main_title">ANNING-LETTER-1</title></titleStmt></egXML>
                    </div>
                   
                   <div>
                       <head xml:id="publicationStmt">Publication information [REQ]</head>
                       <p><gi>publicationStmt</gi> contains information about the person or project who contributed the letter transcription.</p>
                       <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <publicationStmt>
        <authority>Mary Anning Project</authority>
        <date when="2025-03-26">2025</date>
      </publicationStmt>
    </egXML>
                       <p>or</p>
                       <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <publicationStmt>
        <authority>Jane Bloggs</authority>
        <date when="2025-03-26">2025</date>
      </publicationStmt>
    </egXML>
                   </div>
                   
                   <div>
                        <head xml:id="postmarks">Postmarks and datestamps [OPT]</head>
                        <p><!-- Liz, should we support proper correspAction ways of encoding these? -->If
                            an item contains a postmark (or a datestamp in the case of a telegram),
                            it should go in <gi>notesStmt</gi> along with the description,
                            provenance, and related items (if any). The order is not significant.
                            Postmarks and datestamps are notes with types <att>postmark</att> or
                                <att>datestamp</att> respectively:</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <notesStmt>
        <note type="postmark">JA 17 1825</note>
      </notesStmt>
    </egXML>
                    </div>
                   
                   <div>
                        <head xml:id="endorsement">Endorsement [OPT]</head>
                        <p>If a letter has an endorsement, it goes in <gi>notesStmt</gi>, similar to
                            postmarks and datestamps:</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <notesStmt>
        <note type="postmark">JA 17 1825</note>
        <note type="endorsement" subtype="letter">‘/76’</note>
      </notesStmt>
    </egXML>
                    </div>
                   
                   <div>
                       <head xml:id="sourceDesc">The description of the source (<gi>sourceDesc</gi>) [REQ]</head>
                       
                       <div>
                           <head xml:id="provenance">Manuscripts</head>
                           <p>There are two ways that you can record the letter's manuscript source. The preferred method is to use the detailed elements in <gi>msDesc</gi>:</p>
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <msDesc>
          <msIdentifier>
            <repository>The Geological Society</repository>
            <idno>LDGSL/613/2/57</idno>
          </msIdentifier>
        </msDesc>
      </egXML>
                           <list>
                               <item><gi>repository</gi> contains the name of the institution that holds the items.</item>
                               <item><gi>idno</gi> contains that institution's shelfmark or identifying number for the item.</item>
                           </list>
                           <p>You can also use a simpler form:</p>
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <bibl type="provenance">LDGSL/613/2/57, The Geological Society</bibl>
      </egXML>
                       </div>
                       
                       <div>
                           <head xml:id="printedSources">Printed Sources</head>
                           <p><!-- We should probably support biblStruct - or at least a bit more bibl coding -->Currently, the only method to record printed sources is:</p>
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <bibl type="printedSource"><hi rend="italic">LL</hi> 3: 219</bibl>
      </egXML>
                       </div>
                       
                       <div>
                           <head>Letters comprised from multiple sources</head>
                           <p>Letters sometimes come from multiple physical sources—for example,
                                split into different classmarks, contained within institutions or
                                published separately. In such cases, provide separate descriptions
                                of each source in order. The following describes a hypothetical
                                letter where the first part is in The Geological Society and the
                                final part is in the Cambridge University Library:</p>
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <msDesc>
          <msIdentifier>
            <repository>The Geological Society</repository>
            <idno>LDGSL/613/2/57</idno>
          </msIdentifier>
        </msDesc>
        <msDesc>
          <msIdentifier>
            <repository>Cambridge University Library</repository>
            <idno>MS Add 999999999</idno>
          </msIdentifier>
        </msDesc>
      </egXML>
                           <p>or briefly:</p>
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <bibl type="provenance">LDGSL/613/2/57, The Geological Society</bibl>
        <bibl type="provenance">MS Add 999999999, Cambridge University Library</bibl>
      </egXML>
                       </div>
                       
                   </div>
               </div>
               
           </div>
           
               <div><head xml:id="profileDesc">profileDesc [REQ]</head>
                   <p><gi>profileDesc</gi> describes the correspondents and may, optionally, contain a summary of the letter.</p>
                   <div>
                    <head xml:id="correspondent">Recording Correspondence Information [REQ]</head>
                    <p>Information about correspondence is recorded in <gi>correspDesc</gi>, which
                        should contain two <gi>correspAction</gi> elements in the following
                        order:</p>
                    <list>
                        <item><tag>correspAction type="sent"</tag>: Information about the
                            sender.</item>
                        <item><tag>correspAction type="received"</tag>: Information about the
                            recipient.</item>
                    </list>
                    <p>Example structure:</p>
                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <correspAction type="sent">
      <persName>Darwin, C. R.</persName>
      <placeName>Abinger Hall Down</placeName>
      <date when="1875-06-14">14 June [1875]</date>
    </correspAction>
    <correspAction type="received">
      <persName>Schultze, Fritz</persName>
    </correspAction>
  </egXML>
                    <p>Each <gi>correspAction</gi> element may contain the following elements:</p>
                    <list>
                        <item><gi>persName</gi> or <gi>orgName</gi>
                            <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi>: The name of the individual or organization
                            involved (sender or recipient).</item>
                        <item><gi>placeName</gi>
                            <hi rend="bold">[OPT]</hi>: The place associated with the action (the
                            sender’s or recipient’s location).</item>
                        <item><gi>date</gi>
                            <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi> (sender), <hi rend="bold">[OPT]</hi>
                            (recipient): The date associated with the action (the sending or
                            receiving date).</item>
                    </list>
                    <p>Always record the sender first (<tag>correspAction type="sent"</tag>) and
                        then the recipient (<tag>correspAction type="received"</tag>).</p>
                    <div>
                        <head xml:id="altcorrespondent">Alternative Correspondents</head>
                        <p>If the correspondent is <hi rend="bold">either</hi> one person <hi
                                rend="bold">or</hi> another, you should list each possible
                            correspondent within a single <gi>correspAction</gi>, assigning each an
                                <att>xml:id</att> and using the <att>exclude</att> attribute to show
                            mutual exclusivity.</p>
                        <p>For example, if a letter's recipient could be either W. W. Baxter,
                            William Baxter, or William Warner Baxter (each considered a distinct
                            individual as opposed to three different versions of one indvidual’s
                            name), your markup should look like this:</p>
                        <p><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <correspAction type="received" subtype="personGrp">
      <persName xml:id="name1" exclude="#name2 #name3">Baxter, W. W.</persName>
      <persName xml:id="name2" exclude="#name1 #name3">Baxter, William</persName>
      <persName xml:id="name3" exclude="#name1 #name2">Baxter, William Q.</persName>
    </correspAction>
  </egXML></p>
                        <p>To manage mutually exclusive correspondents clearly:</p>
                        <list>
                            <item>Assign a unique <att>xml:id</att> to each potential correspondent.
                                The IDs only need to be unique within the current document (for
                                example, <tag>xml:id="name1"</tag>, <tag>xml:id="name2"</tag>,
                                etc.).</item>
                            <item>Use the <att>exclude</att> attribute to specify mutually exclusive
                                choices. For example,
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><persName xml:id="name1" exclude="#name2 #name3">Baxter, W. W.</persName></egXML>
                                indicates that selecting "name1" excludes "name2" and "name3".
                                Similarly, the second correspondent,
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><persName xml:id="name2" exclude="#name1 #name3">Baxter, William</persName></egXML>,
                                excludes "name1" and "name3"; and the third,
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><persName xml:id="name3" exclude="#name1 #name2">Baxter, William Q.</persName></egXML>,
                                excludes "name1" and "name2".</item>
                        </list>
                        <p>This markup clearly documents alternative correspondents and ensures
                            accurate identification within Epsilon.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <head xml:id="dates">Dates [REQ]</head>
                        <p>Letter dates are recorded using <gi>date</gi> inside
                                <gi>correspAction</gi>.</p>
                        <p>The date the letter is sent should be in <tag>correspAction type
                                ="sent"</tag>; the date it was received should be in
                                <tag>correspAction type ="received"</tag>.</p>
                        <p>The date contains several key pieces of information:</p>
                        <list rend="ordered">
                            <item>A natural-language version of the date is contained within the
                                element (<hi rend="italic">e.g.</hi> “1 Jan 1880”, “after 1 Jan
                                1880”, “22 June [1874?]”):
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">[c. 31 Jan 1878?]</egXML>
                            </item>
                            <item>One or more attributes: <att>when</att>, <att>notBefore</att>,
                                and/or <att>notAfter</att> that contain ISO versions of the date
                                    (<val>yyyy-mm-dd</val>) to facilitate searching and
                                sorting.</item>
                        </list>
                        <p>Use the attributes as follows:</p>
                        <list>
                            <item><att>when</att> for a specific known (or inferred) date:
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date when="1880-01-06">6 Jan 1880</date></egXML>
                            </item>
                            <item><att>notBefore</att> when only the earliest possible date is
                                known:
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notBefore="1879-02-17">[after 16 Feb 1879]</date></egXML>
                            </item>
                            <item><att>notAfter</att> when only the latest possible date is known:
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notAfter="1879-02-15">[before 16 Feb 1879]</date></egXML>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                        <p>It is highly recommended that you establish canonical dates for the
                            earliest and latest possible date a letter could have been written.
                            These dates allow for open-ended ranges containing only a single
                                <att>notBefore</att> or <att>notAfter</att> to work better within
                            Epsilon's larger collection. The Darwin Correspondence Project used
                            Darwin's birth date (1809-01-01) and the end of the year of his death
                            (1882-12-31) as these values. </p>
                        <p>
                            <list>
                                <item><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notBefore="1879-02-17" notAfter="1882-12-31">[after 16 Feb 1879]</date></egXML>
                                </item>
                                <item><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notBefore="1809-01-01" notAfter="1879-02-15">[before 16 Feb 1879]</date></egXML>
                                </item>
                            </list>
                        </p>
                        <p>A letter written over multiple known days should use separate
                                <gi>date</gi> elements (this does not define a range, but two
                            separate events):</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <date when="1880-01-01">1 Jan 1880</date>
    <date when="1880-01-09">9 Jan 1880</date>
  </egXML>
                        <p>A letter written between two dates would be encoded using a single
                                <gi>date</gi> with <att>notBefore</att> and <att>notAfter</att>:</p>
                        <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <date notBefore="1879-12-24" notAfter="1880-01-05">[24 Dec 1879–5 Jan 1880]</date>
  </egXML>
                        <p>Be careful when encoding <att>notBefore</att> and <att>notAfter</att>.
                            The logic of the attributes might differ slightly from the logic of the
                            natural language text:</p>
                        <list>
                            <item><att>notAfter</att> means "on or before" the given date:
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notAfter="1880-01-05">[before 6 Jan 1880]</date></egXML>
                                    (<hi rend="italic">i.e.</hi>, 5 Jan or earlier). </item>
                            <item><att>notBefore</att> means "on or after" the given date:
                                    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notBefore="1880-01-06">[after 5 Jan 1880]</date></egXML>
                                    (<hi rend="italic">i.e.</hi>, 6 Jan or later). </item>
                        </list>
                        <p>For more complex date situations, including exclusive dates, see <ref
                                target="#datesappendix">Appendix I: Dates</ref> at the bottom of
                            this document.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                   <div>
                       <head xml:id="summary">Summary of the letter [OPT]</head>
                       
                       <p>You may supply a summary using <gi>abstract</gi>, which appears
                        immediately after <gi>correspDesc</gi>:</p>
                       
                       <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <abstract>
      <p>Thanks FS for his book [<hi rend="italic">Kant und Darwin</hi>].</p>
      <p>Sends regards to Haeckel.</p>
    </abstract>
  </egXML>
                       
                       
                   </div>
                   
               </div>
           <div>
                <head xml:id="revisionDesc">Putting a Letter Online [REQ]</head>
                <p>Your transcribed letter text will only appear online after you have indicated
                    that it has been cleared for release. This is done inside the
                        <gi>revisionDesc</gi> portion of the header.<gi>revisionDesc</gi>  lists all
                    editorial actions performed on the file, including the cataloging of the
                    metadata, transcription of the text, its proofreading, and online release. Each
                    action is represented as a <gi>change</gi> element, containing:</p>
                <list>
                    <item><att>when</att>
                        <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi> with the date of the action.</item>
                    <item><att>status</att>
                        <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi> indicating the status of the document in the
                        publication pipeline</item>
                    <item>an optional <gi>name</gi> element to indicate the person responsible,
                        identified by an <att>xml:id</att> assigned by senior editors. This
                        identifier typically combines the person's initials and surname, e.g.,
                            ‘<val>jbloggs</val>’, ‘<val>mhawkins</val>’.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Add new <gi>change</gi> elements at the end of the existing list. If a person's
                        <att>xml:id</att> has already been declared in a previous <gi>change</gi>,
                    subsequent references to this person should use the <att>sameAs</att> attribute,
                    referencing the existing <att>xml:id</att> (e.g., <tag>name
                        sameAs="#jbloggs"</tag>).</p>
                <p>The <att>status</att> attribute can take one of these four values:</p>
                <list>
                    <item><val>catalogued</val></item>
                    <item><val>transcribed</val></item>
                    <item><val>proofed</val></item>
                    <item><val>released</val>
                        <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi> to indicate the letter text can be made available
                        on the Epsilon site.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Example of a letter that has just been catalogued:</p>
                <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <revisionDesc>
      <change when="2015-10-02" status="catalogued" xml:id="jbloggs">Catalogued by Jane Bloggs</change>
    </revisionDesc>
  </egXML>
                <p>Example of a letter that has been catalogued, transcribed and proofed:</p>
                <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <change when="2015-10-02" status="catalogued">Catalogued by <name  xml:id="jbloggs">Jane Bloggs</name></change>
<change when="2015-11-02" status="transcribed">Transcribed by <name xml:id="another">A.N. Other</name>.</change>
    <change when="2015-12-02" status="proofed">Document proofed by <name sameAs="#jbloggs">Jane Bloggs</name>.</change>
  </egXML>
            
            <p>To mark a letter as released, simply add a <gi>change</gi> element with
                    <att>status</att> equal to <val>released</val>.</p>
               <p><egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
               <change when="2015-12-03" status="released">Document released.</change>
           </egXML></p>
           <p> This is the only change element that is required for letters. All the others are optional. The textual content of the change elements can be as you like, but they are not displayed on the Epsilon site.</p>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="editing">Editing the Letter Text</head>
               
               <p>The textual content of the letter, including any enclosures, translations, and footnotes, is contained within the <gi>body</gi> element inside <gi>text</gi>. The <gi>body</gi> is divided into three sections, each coded as a separate <gi>div</gi> with a <att>type</att> attribute indicating its content:</p>
               
               <list>
                   <item><tag>div type="letter"</tag>: <hi rend="bold">[REQ]</hi> Contains the
                        original letter content.</item>
                   <item><tag>div type="translation"</tag>: <hi rend="bold">[OPT]</hi> Contains
                        translation content, if one is being provided.</item>
                   <item><tag>div type="footnotes"</tag>: <hi rend="bold">[OPT]</hi> Contains
                        footnotes, if any exist.</item>
               </list>
               
               <p>Example structure:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <text>
      <body>
        <div type="letter">
          <![CDATA[<!-- Letter content goes here -->]]>
        </div>

        <div type="translation">
          <![CDATA[<!-- Translation content goes here, if applicable. Otherwise omit this div. -->]]>
        </div>

        <div type="footnotes">
          <![CDATA[<!-- Footnotes go here, if applicable. Otherwise omit this div. -->]]>
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>Only the <tag>div type="letter"</tag> is mandatory. Include <tag>div type="translation"</tag> and <tag>div type="footnotes"</tag> only if the letter has translations or footnotes, respectively. Otherwise, omit these elements.</p>
           </div>
           <div>
               <head xml:id="opener">Opener [REC]</head>
               
               <p>The opener contains the address (<gi>placeName</gi>), date (<gi>date</gi>),
                    salutation (<gi>salute</gi>), and any salutation notes (<tag>seg
                        type="supplemental"</tag>).</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <opener>
      <placeName><hi rend="print">Down, | Beckenham, Kent. | Railway Station | Orpington. S.E.R.</hi></placeName>
      <date>Sunday</date>
      <seg type="supplemental">(Home tomorrow)</seg>
      <salute>Dear Dyer.</salute>
    </opener>
  </egXML>
               <div>
                   <head xml:id="address">Address [OPT]</head>
                   <p>The address is encoded using <gi>placeName</gi> and should be coded exactly as it appears in the letter. Use <tag>hi rend="print"</tag> if the address is printed letterhead.</p>
               
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
                   <opener>
                   <placeName><hi rend="print">The Forest</hi>.</placeName>
                   <date>Monday Feb<hi rend="superscript">y</hi>. 18<hi rend="superscript">th</hi>.—</date>
                   <salute>My dear Charles,</salute>
               </opener>
               </egXML>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head xml:id="date">Date [OPT]</head>
                   <p>The <gi>date</gi> element within <gi>opener</gi> records the date exactly as it appears in the manuscript or printed source. Dating attributes are not required here. Indicate any printed portions of the date with <tag>hi rend="print"</tag>, and superscripts with <tag>hi rend="superscript"</tag>.</p>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head xml:id="supplemental">Salutation note/Sendname [OPT]</head>
                   <p>Supplemental text in the header should now be marked as <tag>seg type="supplemental"</tag>. It will be left-justified above the salutation in printed form.</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <seg type="supplemental">M<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. Charles Darwin in Down, Beckenham, Kent, England.</seg>
    </egXML>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head xml:id="salutation">Salutation</head>
                   <p>The salutation remains unchanged, encoded simply as <gi>salute</gi>:</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <salute>Dear Sir,</salute>
    </egXML>
               </div>
               
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="text">Letter Body Text</head>
               
               <p>Prose paragraphs should be marked with <gi>p</gi>. Normally, minor variations in paragraph formatting from the original source don't need encoding. However, specific block formatting such as centering, right justification, unusual first-line indentation, hanging indents, or block quotations should be indicated using the <att>rend</att> attribute.</p>
               
               <p>If text appears centered because it functions as a heading, tag it as a heading rather than a centered paragraph. In this case, use <tag>ab rend="centre" type="head"</tag> instead of <tag>p rend="centre"</tag>.</p>
               
               <!--TODO: Provide detailed guidelines on acceptable values for the <att>rend</att> attribute for various block formats (e.g., left justification, indents, etc.)-->
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="footnoteRefs">Footnote Markers</head>
               
               <p>Footnote markers should be encoded using <gi>ref</gi> as follows:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
                   ...Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.<ref target="#foot.f2"/> Quisque vestibulum urna sed justo gravida...
  </egXML>
               
               <p>Here, the value “<val>#foot.f2</val>” points to a corresponding note at the bottom of the document, marked with an <att>xml:id</att> of “<val>foot.f2</val>”.</p>
               
               <p>Footnotes themselves appear as <gi>note</gi> elements within the final <gi>div</gi> in the <gi>body</gi>, tagged as <tag>div type="footnotes"</tag>:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <note xml:id="foot.f1">Lorem ipse dolor zzrill</note>
    <note xml:id="foot.f2">See letter to William Marshall, 29 May 1875. CD had sent Marshall an ostrich feather that he had received from Thomas François Burgers.</note>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>You do not need to duplicate <gi>note</gi> elements for footnotes with identical content in both the original letter and its translation. Only create a new <gi>note</gi> for the translation if <hi rend="bold">no</hi> existing <gi>note</gi> matches the desired content.</p>
               
               <p>Ensure each footnote reference within the letter or translation correctly points to the appropriate footnote using the <tag>ref</tag> element, e.g., <tag>ref target="#foot.f12"</tag>. You don't need to manually add footnote numbers; Epsilon will automatically number notes and their markers when displayed.</p>
               
               <p>Footnote markers can also be applied to certain metadata elements within the header, such as <tag>note type="description"</tag>, <tag>note type="postmark"</tag>, <tag>note type="endorsement"</tag>, and <tag>bibl type="provenance"</tag>.</p>
               
           </div>
           
           <div>
                <head xml:id="x-refs">Letter and Person Cross References</head>
                <p>Letter cross-references in footnotes should be encoded so they link directly to
                    the referenced letter. Wrap the descriptive text of the referenced letter (e.g.,
                    ‘letter from E. A. Darwin, 5 [March 1823]’) in a <gi>ref</gi> element. This
                    element must have a <att>target</att> attribute containing a relative path that
                    points to another TEI resource within your repository. For example:</p>
                <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    See also the <ref target="./DCP-LETT-5.xml">letter from E. A. Darwin, 5 [March 1823]</ref>.
  </egXML>
                <p>Simply put, this means it shows the location of the document you're linking to in
                    relation to the current document you're working on. For instance, <val>./</val>
                    means the referenced file is in the same folder as your current file. A path
                    like <val>../</val> means the file is located one folder up from your current
                    location. If you are linking from one letter file (say
                        <val>ANNING-LETTER-1.xml</val> to another, say
                        <val>ANNING-LETTER-2.xml</val>, you would the target would be
                        <val>./ANNING-LETTER-2.xml</val>.</p>
                <p>The examples above assume you're linking between items of the same type (e.g.,
                    letter to letter, person to person). If you're linking between different types
                    of documents (such as from a letter to a person), include the full relative
                    path. For instance, linking from a letter file to a person entry (located in
                    another folder) might look like this:</p>
                <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    ... <ref target="../nameregs/nameregs_1.xml">Charlotte Murchison</ref> ...
  </egXML>
            </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="list">Lists</head>
               <p>Lists should be encoded using <tag>list rend="ordered"</tag>, with each item in
                    the list tagged as <gi>item</gi>. If a list is numbered, transcribe the number
                    and its punctuation exactly as they appear in the manuscript, marking them as
                        <gi>label</gi>:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <list rend="ordered">
      <item><label>1.</label> “intense cobalt Blue”</item>
      <item><label>2</label> “silver yellow”</item>
      <item><label>4)</label> “deep copper red”.</item>
    </list>
  </egXML>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="whitespace">Intentionally Blank Whitespace in the Original Document</head>
               <p>Instead of using line breaks or strings of spaces to represent intentionally created whitespace in the original letter, use the <gi>space</gi> element. This element has three attributes:</p>
               <list>
                   <item><att>dim</att> specifies the direction (‘<val>horizontal</val>’ or ‘<val>vertical</val>’).</item>
                   <item><att>extent</att> gives a numerical value indicating how large the space is.</item>
                   <item><att>unit</att> indicates the unit of measurement (typically ‘<val>chars</val>’ for horizontal whitespace or ‘<val>lines</val>’ for vertical whitespace).</item>
               </list>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="specialCharacters">Special Characters</head>
               <p>Most special characters encountered in letters, such as those used in Western
                    European languages, can be entered directly using the Character Map. However,
                    certain characters, like less-than or greater-than signs (&lt;, &gt;),
                    ampersands (&amp;), male/female symbols, and the symbols for degrees, minutes,
                    and seconds, should be entered using named entities as outlined in Appendix
                    XXXXXXX. A full list of available entities can be found in our <ref
                        target="/view?docId=documentation/EntityList.xml">Entity List</ref>.</p>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="mathematics">Mathematics, Fractions, and Equations</head>
               <p>Fractions and equations should be marked up using representational MathML.
                    Examples can be found in the comprehensive guidelines linked from the
                    introduction. Here's an example of encoding the fraction one half (½):</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <formula>
      <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
        <mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac>
      </math>
    </formula>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>If you need to encode more complex mathematical content, please contact <ref target="mailto:epsilon@lib.cam.ac.uk">epsilon@lib.cam.ac.uk</ref></p>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="formatting">Character Formatting</head>
               
               <p>Text that has distinctive formatting, such as <hi rend="bold">bold</hi>, <hi rend="italic">italicised</hi>, <hi rend="subscript">subscripted</hi>, <hi rend="superscript">superscripted</hi>, <hi rend="smallCaps">Small Capitals</hi>, or <hi rend="underline">underlined</hi> should be tagged using the <gi>hi</gi> element with a corresponding <att>rend</att> attribute. Possible values include: </p>
                   <list>
                       <item><val>bold</val>: <hi rend="bold">bold text</hi></item>
                       <item><val>italic</val>: <hi rend="italic">italicised text</hi></item>
                       <item><val>subscript</val>: Lorem<hi rend="subscript">subscripted</hi></item>
                       <item><val>superscript</val>: Lorem<hi rend="superscript">superscripted</hi></item>
                       <item><val>smallCaps</val>: <hi rend="smallCaps">Text in Small Capitals</hi></item>
                       <item><val>doubleUnderline</val>: <hi rend="doubleUnderline">double underlining</hi></item>
                       <item><val>underline</val>: <hi rend="underline">underline</hi></item>
                   </list>
                   
                   <p>A full list of available values is provided in the Element Set documentation.</p>
               
               <p>Multiple formatting styles can be applied simultaneously. For example, text that is both superscripted and italicised would be encoded as follows:</p>
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <hi rend="superscript italic">superscripted italic text</hi>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>Formatting is inherited when nested. For example, if you have superscripted text with only a portion in italics, encode it as:</p>
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <hi rend="superscript">superscripted <hi rend="italic">italic</hi> text</hi>
  ]</egXML>
               
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="diagrams">Diagrams and Illustrations</head>
               
               <p>TODO</p>
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <figure><graphic/></figure>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>The <gi>graphic</gi> element will eventually include a <att>url</att> attribute pointing to the actual image file, but you don't need to worry about this during the normal editing process. Until an image file is provided and linked, the markup above will display automatically as “[DIAG HERE]”.</p>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="enclosures">Enclosures [OPT]</head>
               
               <p>If a letter includes an enclosure, encode it within a <tag>div type="enclosure"</tag> element, placed directly below the <tag>div type="text"</tag>. Each enclosure must include a mandatory <att>subtype</att> attribute specifying whether the enclosure is a <val>letter</val> or <val>other</val>. <hi rend="bold">Each enclosure must also contain a <tag>div type="transcription"</tag> element</hi> for the enclosed text to appear correctly in Epsilon.</p>
               
               <p>If multiple enclosures are present, create a separate <tag>div type="enclosure"</tag> for each one, assigning each enclosure a unique <att>xml:id</att>. It is essential that the enclosures are nested correctly inside the main <tag>div type="letter"</tag>.</p>
               
               <p>If there is an enclosure but no main letter text is available, you still need to include the standard empty structure (including the <gi>text</gi> element), even if it is left blank.</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <div type="enclosure" subtype="letter" xml:id="encl.1">
        <![CDATA[<!-- Transcription of a single enclosure (an enclosed letter). -->]]>
        <opener>
            <![CDATA[<!-- Salutation and other opening elements of enclosed letter -->]]>
        </opener>
        <div type="transcription">
            <![CDATA[<!-- Main body text of enclosed letter -->]]>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div type="enclosure" subtype="other" xml:id="encl.2">
        <![CDATA[<!-- Transcription of another type of enclosure (not a letter). -->]]>
        <div type="transcription">
            <![CDATA[<!-- Main body text of non-letter enclosure -->]]>
        </div>
    </div>
  </egXML>
               
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="footnotes">Footnotes</head>
               
               <p>Footnotes are placed within a <tag>div type="footnotes"</tag> element. Each footnote should be created as a <gi>note</gi> element, assigned a unique <att>xml:id</att>. These IDs are referenced by the <att>target</att> attribute of <gi>ref</gi> elements elsewhere in the document. For example, the footnote with ID ‘<val>foot.f1</val>’ would be referenced as <tag>ref target="#foot.f1"</tag>:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
    <note xml:id="foot.f1">The year is established by the relationship between this letter and the letter from John Scott, [1-11] April [1863].</note>
  </egXML>
               
               <p>Ensure the footnotes' <gi>div</gi> (<tag>div type="footnotes"</tag>) is not nested inside any other <gi>div</gi>. It must be the last element inside the <gi>body</gi> element, placed after all other <gi>div</gi> elements.</p>
               
               <p>You should not provide footnote numbers in the text or the notes themselves as the footnotes and their indicators will be automatically numbered according to their final position within the document when displayed online. The primary requirement is that each <gi>ref</gi> correctly points to the appropriate <gi>note</gi> via the <att>target</att> attribute (e.g., <tag>ref target="#foot.f1"</tag> points to <tag>note xml:id="foot.f1"</tag>). While editors should choose semantically meaningful <att>xml:id</att> values for notes (like foot.f1, foot.f2, foot.f3), the exact text used in these IDs doesn't affect final numbering. Technically, the IDs could even be arbitrary (e.g., ‘larry’, ‘moe’, and ‘curly’); all that matters is accurate referencing between each <gi>ref</gi> and its corresponding <gi>note</gi>.</p>
           </div>
           
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="datesappendix">Appendix I: Correspondence Dates</head>
               
               <p>Correspodence dates should be encoded using the <gi>date</gi> element, which has two main parts:</p>
               
               <list>
                   <item>The natural-language text inside the element (<hi rend="italic">e.g.</hi>, "1 Jan 1880"; "after 1 Jan 1880"; "22 June 1874–82").</item>
                   <item>One or two attributes (<att>when</att>, <att>notBefore</att>, and/or <att>notAfter</att>) that contain ISO-formatted date values (<val>yyyy-mm-dd</val>), used for sorting and searching letters.</item>
               </list>
               
               <div>
                   <head>a) Encoding the Natural-Language Date Text</head>
                   <p>Enter dates exactly as you would normally present them in print (e.g., "1 Jan 1880"; "after 1 Jan 1880"; "before 1 Jan 1880"; "1-10 Jan 1880"; "Oct–Dec 1846"; "22 June 1874–82").</p>
                   <!-- TODO Do we want to support supplied in this fashion? -->
                   <p>If part of the date is inferred, this is often indicated using square brackets, mark it with the <gi>supplied</gi> element, giving the name of the editor who inferred it using <att>resp</att>. If there's uncertainty, also add a <att>cert</att> attribute ("low", "medium", or "high"). Examples:</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date>22 June <supplied resp="#mjhawkins">1874–82</supplied></date>
</egXML>
                   
                   <p>This renders as "22 June [1874–82]". With uncertainty (represented by a question mark):</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date><supplied resp="#mjhawkins" cert="medium"><hi rend="italic">c.</hi> 31 January 1878</supplied></date>
</egXML>
                   
                   <p>This renders as "[<hi rend="italic">c.</hi> 31 January 1878?]".</p>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head>b) ISO Date Attributes: <att>when</att>, <att>notBefore</att>, <att>notAfter</att></head>
                   <list>
                       <item><att>when</att>: Used when a letter has a specific known or inferred date:
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date when="1880-01-06">6 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                       </item>
                       
                       <item><att>notBefore</att>: Indicates the earliest possible date the letter was written:
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date notBefore="1880-01-07">after 6 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                       </item>
                       
                       <item><att>notAfter</att>: Indicates the latest possible date the letter was written:
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date notAfter="1880-01-05">before 6 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                       </item>
                       
                       <item>When used together, <att>notBefore</att> and <att>notAfter</att> define a date range:
                           <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date notBefore="1879-12-24" notAfter="1880-01-05">24 December 1879 – 5 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                       </item>
                   </list>
                   
                   <p><hi rend="bold">Important:</hi> Pay careful attention to the meaning of your natural language and attribute values when encoding date ranges. Don't just convert the natural language date to its ISO equivalent without carefully considering the logic.</p>
                   <p>If the textual date says "before 6 Jan 1880", the correct ISO date in <att>notAfter</att> is one day earlier: <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notAfter="1880-01-05">[before 6 Jan 1880]</date></egXML>. That is, the letter could not have been written after 5 Jan 1880 or, put another way, it was written before 6 Jan.</p>
                   <p>If the letter was written "after 6 Jan 1880, you would use <att>notBefore</att>: <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples"><date notBefore="1880-01-07">[after 6 Jan 1880]</date></egXML></p>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head>c) Multiple or Mutually Exclusive Dates</head>
                   <p>If there are multiple possible dates that are mutually exclusive (for example, uncertainty between two or more dates), encode each option separately using unique <att>xml:id</att> attributes and indicate mutual exclusion with <att>exclude</att>. Example:</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date when="1880-01-16" xml:id="date1" exclude="#date2">16 January 1880</date>
<date when="1880-01-18" xml:id="date2" exclude="#date1">18 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                   
                   <p>This means the letter was written on either 16 or 18 January 1880, but not both. For more complex examples (e.g., multiple possible Wednesdays in a month), extend the logic similarly:</p>
                   
                   <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<date when="1880-01-07" xml:id="date1" exclude="#date2 #date3 #date4">7 January 1880</date>
<date when="1880-01-14" xml:id="date2" exclude="#date1 #date3 #date4">14 January 1880</date>
<date when="1880-01-21" xml:id="date3" exclude="#date1 #date2 #date4">21 January 1880</date>
<date when="1880-01-28" xml:id="date4" exclude="#date1 #date2 #date3">28 January 1880</date>
</egXML>
                   
                   <p>If one date is more probable, use the <att>cert</att> attribute, marking the more likely option with "<val>high</val>" and the others with "<val>low</val>".</p>
                   
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head>d) Complex Dates and Help</head>
                   <p>Because encoding complex or multiple dates is prone to mistakes, there's a reference file (0-dates) with pre-made snippets for multiple mutually exclusive date scenarios (two to twelve options). Copy and modify these snippets as needed. If more than twelve options are required or if you have questions, contact Epsilon.</p>
                   <!-- TODO: SHould we provide a file that contains two, three, four, five ... 31 alternating dates? That'd cover the more common situations - ie some day in month X YYYY; Day X in some month in YYYY. -->
                   <!-- I could likely create a javascript widget that could create any desired number of options -->
                   
                   <p>The key takeaway is to think carefully about the meaning of dates in natural language and translate that clearly and precisely into the correct TEI attributes—avoid copying dates directly into attributes without thought.</p>
               </div>
           </div>
           
           <div>
               <head xml:id="tablesappendix">Appendix II: Tables</head>
               
               <p>Tables are encoded using the elements <gi>table</gi>, <gi>row</gi>, and <gi>cell</gi>.</p>
               
               <p>The <gi>table</gi> element can optionally have a <att>rend</att> attribute to define its width relative to the browser viewport. Valid values range from ‘<val>width05</val>’ up to ‘<val>width100</val>’ in increments of 5, as well as ‘<val>width33</val>’ and ‘<val>width66</val>’.</p>
               
               <p>The <gi>cell</gi> element can also have an optional <att>rend</att> attribute, with values specifying:</p>
               <list>
                   <item>Alignment: ‘<val>left</val>’, ‘<val>right</val>’, ‘<val>centre</val>’.</item>
                   <item>Borders: ‘<val>borderAll</val>’, ‘<val>borderBottom</val>’, ‘<val>borderLeft</val>’, ‘<val>borderRight</val>’, ‘<val>borderSides</val>’, ‘<val>borderTop</val>’.</item>
                   <item>Width relative to the table: similar increments to the table widths described above.</item>
               </list>
               
               <p>The following example creates a table that occupies 50% of the viewport width. The first two columns each take up 20% of the table's width, and the last column occupies the remaining 60%:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<table rend="width50">
  <row>
    <cell rend="width20">A: One</cell>
    <cell rend="width20">A: Two</cell>
    <cell rend="width60">A: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell>B: One</cell>
    <cell>B: Two</cell>
    <cell>B: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell>C: One</cell>
    <cell>C: Two</cell>
    <cell>C: Three</cell>
  </row>
</table>
</egXML>
               
               <p>Every <gi>row</gi> in a table must have the same total number of columns, but this doesn’t always mean having the same number of actual <gi>cell</gi> elements. Sometimes, a cell will span across multiple columns or rows. To do this, you use the <att>cols</att> or <att>rows</att> attributes. For example, if a table normally has four columns and you use <tag>cell cols="3"</tag> in one of the cells, that row will contain only two <gi>cell</gi> elements: one spanning three columns and one normal cell. However, the total logical number of columns (cells plus their spans) must still equal four. The <att>rows</att> attribute works similarly, except it spans cells vertically, taking up space in the specified number of rows beneath it.</p>
               
               <p>A cell spanning three columns is coded as:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<cell cols="3">...</cell>
</egXML>
               
               <p>Similarly, a cell spanning three columns is encoded as:</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<cell rows="3">...</cell>
</egXML>
               
               <p>Example demonstrating row spanning (vertical spanning):</p>
               
               <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
<table>
  <row>
    <cell>A: One</cell>
    <cell rend="width10" rows="3">Narrow cell spanning three rows</cell>
    <cell>A: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell>B: One</cell>
    <![CDATA[<!-- 'Phantom cell' created by rows="3" above -->]]>
    <cell>B: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell>C: One</cell>
    <![CDATA[<!-- Another 'phantom cell' -->]]>
    <cell>C: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell>D: One</cell>
    <cell>D: Two</cell>
    <cell>D: Three</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell cols="3">One wide cell spanning three columns</cell>
  </row>
</table>
</egXML>
               
               <div>
                   <head>Stretchy Brackets in Tables</head>
                   <p>You can create stretchy brackets in tables using a single character wrapped in <gi>hi</gi> with a <att>rend</att> of either ‘<val>stretchyVertical</val>’ (for vertical brackets spanning multiple rows) or ‘<val>stretchyHorizontal</val>’ (for horizontal brackets spanning multiple columns).</p>
                   
                   <p>For vertical brackets:</p>
                   <list rend="simple">
                       <item>The cell must span more than one row using <att>rows</att>.</item>
                       <item>Contain only a single <tag>hi rend="stretchyVertical"</tag>.</item>
                       <item>The <gi>hi</gi> must contain a bracket from the <ref target="#stretchies">stretchy brackets list below</ref>.</item>
                   </list>
                   
                   <p>For horizontal brackets:</p>
                   <list rend="simple">
                       <item>The table must have a specified width.</item>
                       <item>The cell must span more than one column using <att>cols</att>.</item>
                       <item>Contain only a single <tag>hi rend="stretchyHorizontal"</tag>.</item>
                       <item>Use a bracket from the <ref target="#stretchies">stretchy brackets list below</ref>.</item>
                   </list>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <table xml:id="stretchies">
                       <row>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre"><hi rend="bold">Brace type</hi></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre"><hi rend="bold">Left facing</hi></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre"><hi rend="bold">Right facing</hi></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre"><hi rend="bold">Upward facing</hi></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre"><hi rend="bold">Downward facing</hi></cell>
                       </row>
                       <row>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">Curly</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">{</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">}</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⏞ or <ident>&amp;#x23DE;</ident></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⏟ or <ident>&amp;#x23DF;</ident></cell>
                       </row>
                       <row>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">Rounded</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">(</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">)</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⏜ or <ident>&amp;#x23DC;</ident></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⏝ or <ident>&amp;#x23DD;</ident></cell>
                       </row>
                       <row>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">Square</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">[</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">]</cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⎴ or <ident>&amp;#x23B4;</ident></cell>
                           <cell rend="borderAll centre">⎵ or <ident>&amp;#x23B5;</ident></cell>
                       </row>
                   </table>
               </div>
               
               <div>
                   <head>Borders and Vertical Alignment</head>
                   <p>Borders can be applied to tables, rows, or cells using <att>rend</att> attributes (‘<val>borderAll</val>’, ‘<val>borderBottom</val>’, etc.). Adjacent borders automatically collapse into a single border.</p>
                   
                   <p>Cells spanning multiple rows can have vertical alignment controlled via <att>rend</att> with values ‘<val>vAlignTop</val>’, ‘<val>vAlignMiddle</val>’ (default), or ‘<val>vAlignBottom</val>’.</p>
               
               
               <p><hi rend="bold">Note:</hi> If you need additional formatting options (e.g., double borders), please contact Mike to implement custom <att>rend</att> values.</p>
               </div>
           </div>
           
           <!-- Add more docs here -->
           <div xml:id="element-set">
               <schemaSpec ident="tei_math" prefix="tei_" start="TEI" defaultExceptions="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0 http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml teix:egXML">
                   <moduleRef url="https://www.tei-c.org/release/xml/tei/Exemplars/mathml2-main.rng"/>
                   <moduleRef n="01" key="tei"/>
                   <moduleRef n="02" key="header" include="abstract authority availability change correspAction correspDesc edition editionStmt editorialDecl encodingDesc extent fileDesc funder idno keywords langUsage language licence notesStmt profileDesc publicationStmt revisionDesc seriesStmt sourceDesc teiHeader textClass titleStmt">
                       <!--Unused elements: appInfo application biblFull cRefPattern calendar calendarDesc catDesc catRef category citeData citeStructure classCode classDecl conversion correction correspContext creation distributor geoDecl handNote hyphenation interpretation listChange listPrefixDef namespace normalization prefixDef principal projectDesc punctuation quotation refState refsDecl rendition samplingDecl schemaRef scriptNote segmentation sponsor stdVals styleDefDecl tagUsage tagsDecl taxonomy unitDecl unitDef xenoData-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="03" key="core" include="abbr add analytic author bibl biblScope biblStruct cb choice corr date del expan foreign gap graphic head hi imprint item l lb lg list measure monogr name note p pb ptr pubPlace publisher quote ref relatedItem resp respStmt sic term title unclear">
                       <!--Unused elements: addrLine address binaryObject cit citedRange desc distinct divGen editor ellipsis email emph gb gloss headItem headLabel index label listBibl measureGrp media meeting mentioned milestone noteGrp num orig postBox postCode q rb reg rs rt ruby said series soCalled sp speaker stage street teiCorpus textLang time unit-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="04" key="textstructure" include="TEI back body closer div opener salute signed text">
                       <!--Unused elements: argument byline dateline div1 div2 div3 div4 div5 div6 div7 docAuthor docDate docEdition docImprint docTitle epigraph floatingText front group imprimatur postscript titlePage titlePart trailer-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="10" key="msdescription" include="altIdentifier collection institution msContents msDesc msIdentifier msItem msName msPart physDesc repository signatures">
                       <!--Unused elements: accMat acquisition additional additions adminInfo binding bindingDesc catchwords collation colophon condition custEvent custodialHist decoDesc decoNote depth dim dimensions explicit filiation finalRubric foliation handDesc height heraldry history incipit layout layoutDesc locus locusGrp material msFrag msItemStruct musicNotation objectDesc objectType origDate origPlace origin provenance recordHist rubric scriptDesc seal sealDesc secFol source stamp summary support supportDesc surrogates typeDesc typeNote watermark width-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="11" key="transcr" include="ex facsimile handNotes handShift space subst supplied surface">
                       <!--Unused elements: addSpan am damage damageSpan delSpan fw line listTranspose metamark mod path redo restore retrace secl sourceDoc substJoin surfaceGrp surplus transpose undo zone-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="12" key="textcrit">
                       <!--Unused elements: app lacunaEnd lacunaStart lem listApp listWit rdg rdgGrp variantEncoding wit witDetail witEnd witStart witness-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="13" key="namesdates" include="country forename orgName persName placeName roleName settlement surname">
                       <!--Unused elements: addName affiliation age birth bloc climate death district education event faith floruit genName geo geogFeat geogName langKnowledge langKnown listEvent listNym listObject listOrg listPerson listPlace listRelation location nameLink nationality nym object objectIdentifier objectName occupation offset org persPronouns person personGrp persona place population region relation residence sex socecStatus state terrain trait-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="14" key="figures" include="cell figure formula row table">
                       <!--Unused elements: figDesc notatedMusic-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   <moduleRef n="16" key="linking" include="ab anchor seg">
                       <!--Unused elements: alt altGrp annotation join joinGrp link linkGrp listAnnotation standOff timeline when-->
                   </moduleRef>
                   
                   <!--REMOVE UNUSED MODEL CLASSES-->
                   <classSpec ident="model.applicationLike" type="model" mode="delete"/>
